# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored and must never be committed.

# ── Music Assistant ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Prefer an IP ADDRESS or a name your DNS can answer for. A `.local` name is mDNS, and a
# bridge-networked container has no mDNS resolver — it will resolve on the laptop you write
# this file on and fail inside the container. The failure is quiet and slow: the server boots,
# serves the crate, and only the daily curation run fails, so the review queue drains as albums
# are approved and nothing refills it. The boot log now says so explicitly; this note exists so
# you never see that line.
MA_HOST=musicassistant.example.lan
MA_PORT=8095
# Long-lived token for a dedicated NON-ADMIN user (role=user).
# Create one with: node --env-file=.env scripts/ma-user.ts  (see the header there).
# MA's web UI cannot create non-admin users — auth/user/create is API-only.
# NOTE: expires after 365 days despite what the docs claim.
MA_TOKEN=
# Optional. An admin token, used ONLY by scripts/ma-user.ts when changing player_filter,
# so the kiosk's own credential cannot widen its own access.
MA_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# ── Output targets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MA player IDs. Path A is the wired local speakers, path B the network speaker.
PLAYER_ID_PRIMARY=
PLAYER_ID_FALLBACK=
# Ceiling applied in our UI, 0-100. Must sit at or below MA's own max_volume
# for the player, and must be set from an SPL measurement at the pillow.
VOLUME_CEILING=50
VOLUME_START=15

# ── Rooms the child may send music to ────────────────────────────────
# Comma-separated MA player IDs, available only when idle and in daytime hours.
EXTRA_PLAYER_IDS=

# ── ListenBrainz scrobbling ──────────────────────────────────────────
# A token from https://listenbrainz.org/settings/ for a GENERIC HOUSEHOLD account.
#
# READ THIS BEFORE FILLING IT IN. ListenBrainz has no private listens and never has: it is a
# deliberate design decision, listens are CC0, and they are published in weekly public data
# dumps. Anything submitted here is permanently public. That is why the account is a generic
# household one and never the child's, and why NEITHER THE ACCOUNT NAME NOR THIS TOKEN may
# appear anywhere in this repository — naming it here would relink what the generic account
# exists to separate. Empty disables submission entirely. See docs/research/07.
LISTENBRAINZ_TOKEN=

# ── Curation ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The worker runs INSIDE the container, once a day after 03:00 local, and fills the review
# queue at /admin. It decides nothing: everything it finds waits for a person. Run it by hand
# with `docker compose exec platebunken pb curate --write`.
#
# Ceiling on how many albums one run may add. The queue is reviewed by a parent on a phone,
# so a run that adds two hundred candidates is a run that stops the queue being reviewed.
CURATE_MAX=24

# How many daily backups of the crate to keep, beside the store inside the volume. The server
# takes one a day after 04:00 local — after the curation run, so the backup contains the day's
# suggestions. 0 keeps every backup forever.
#
# These live IN the volume on purpose: that protects against the failure that actually happens
# (a bad migration, a mistaken withdrawal, a wrong `pb seed`), and whatever already backs up
# the volume now captures point-in-time copies for free. Losing the volume itself is the
# host's job, not this one's.
BACKUP_KEEP=7

# LISTENBRAINZ_LABS_BASE and NEW_SHELF_TRICKLE_PER_DAY used to live here and are gone. The
# Labs URL is a constant in src/curate/sources.ts and never varied; the trickle rate is a
# stored setting the parent changes at /admin, and having it here as well meant a redeploy
# silently disagreed with the screen. A key nothing reads is the same lie as a toggle that
# does nothing.

# NOT IMPLEMENTED YET. Last.fm is an intended suggestion source (ARCHITECTURE.md §5) and its
# toggle at /admin is drawn as "not built". Nothing reads this; leave it empty.
LASTFM_API_KEY=

# ── Home Assistant / MQTT ────────────────────────────────────────────
# NOT IMPLEMENTED YET (ARCHITECTURE.md §12.8). No code reads any of these. They are listed
# because the design calls for them, not because setting them does anything. Leave them empty
# — an operator chasing broker credentials for a feature that does not exist is a real cost.
MQTT_HOST=
MQTT_PORT=1883
MQTT_USER=
MQTT_PASSWORD=
MQTT_DISCOVERY_PREFIX=homeassistant

# ── The parent gate ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Four digits in front of the settings screen on the device AND the review queue at /admin.
# A CHILD GATE, NOT SECURITY: it is compared in front-end JavaScript and is readable by anyone
# who opens the page source. It stops a four-year-old. The real boundaries are the kiosk
# lockdown, the LAN, and the fact that no credential ever reaches a page.
GATE_PIN=1234

# ── The kiosk machine ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The address reserved for the laptop on the router (a DHCP reservation, not a static set on
# the host). Used only to display it in the parent's settings screen and to let Ansible check
# the reservation took. Example only — the real one lives in .env.
KIOSK_IP=10.0.0.0

# ── The server ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where platebunken-server listens. In the container these three are fixed by compose and by
# the image layout; they are here for `npm run serve` on a dev machine.
PORT=8080
HOST=0.0.0.0
PUBLIC_DIR=./dist/public

# ── Read by compose, not by the app ──────────────────────────────────
# These three configure the DEPLOYMENT rather than the program. The container never sees the
# first two at all.

# Which release to run. `latest` follows the newest semver tag and never `main`.
PLATEBUNKEN_TAG=latest

# The port PUBLISHED ON THE HOST. The container always listens on 8080; this is what the host
# maps it to, and it lives here because which ports are free is a property of the machine, not
# of this product. **Check it is free before deploying:**
#
#   curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' http://HOST:8080/
#
# Anything but an empty answer means something already holds it. On the first host this was
# deployed to, 8080 was another service's — `up -d` fails with "port is already allocated",
# and a reverse proxy or DNS record aimed at the wrong number fails later and far more quietly.

PLATEBUNKEN_PORT=8080

# The container defaults to UTC, and "the new shelf arrives in the morning" is a statement
# about the child's morning.
TZ=Europe/Oslo

# ── Storage ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# On the Docker host this is /data/platebunken.sqlite inside the container, which is a named
# volume. THAT VOLUME IS THE PRODUCT: it holds the child's crate positions, append-only, and
# `docker compose down -v` is the one command that destroys them. See ARCHITECTURE.md §3.1.
DATABASE_PATH=./data/platebunken.sqlite

# Whose crate. Opaque id — never a child's name, this file's example is committed.
# A second profile exists so the older child can be added without renumbering the first
# child's crate, which the database refuses to allow. See docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §5.1.
PROFILE_ID=child_a
PROFILE_LABEL=child_a

# Seed playlist: the curated starting point for the crate. The playlist IS the act of
# approval — it is the one path into the crate that does not pass the review queue, because
# the parent built it by hand. §5.
#
# REQUIRED ON THE DEPLOYED HOST, not just in development. `pb seed` runs inside the container
# against the real volume; when this was a script outside the image it was reasonable to call
# these two "script-only" and leave them out of the host's .env, and that is exactly how the
# only command that can build a crate became unreachable from the only machine that has one.
SEED_PLAYLIST_ID=
SEED_PLAYLIST_PROVIDER=qobuz
